\title{Naïve recursive Fibonacci}
\date{2026-10-27T13:46:25Z}
\tag{algorithms}

\import{algorithms-macros}


\p{Recall the definition of the \em{Fibonacci numbers}:

\transclude{00OZ}

We can directly turn this definition into a recursive function to compute the #{n}th Fibonacci number, but it is extremely slow (in fact, it is #{\Theta(\varphi^n)}):

\pre\verb<<<|
# Fibonacci #1: naive recursive algorithm
def fib1(n):
    if n <= 1:
        return n
    else:
        return fib1(n-1) + fib1(n-2)
<<<}

\p{Why is it so slow?  If we draw the recursion call tree for, say, \code{fib1(5)}, we can see the problem:

\diagram{500}{*}{\verb<<<|
import Diagrams.TwoD.Layout.Tree

fibCalls :: Integer -> BTree Integer
fibCalls 0 = leaf 0
fibCalls 1 = leaf 1
fibCalls n = BNode n (fibCalls (n-1)) (fibCalls (n-2))

Just t = uniqueXLayout 2 2 (fibCalls 5)

dia :: Diagram B
dia = pad 1.1 . centerXY
        $ renderTree
            (\n -> (text (show n)
                    <> roundedRect 1.3 1.3 0.3 # fc gold)
            )
            (~~) t
        `atop` square 1 # scaleY 12 # translateY (-5)
                        # scaleX 34
                        # lw none # fc whitesmoke
<<<}

\code{fib1(5)} calls \code{fib1(4)} and \code{fib1(3)}; but \code{fib1(4)} again calls \code{fib1(3)}; and so on.  \code{fib1(1)} ends up being called five times!  If we were to start with a bigger number, it would be even worse.
}

\p{The problem, then, is that \code{fib1} spends a lot of time redundantly recomputing values that have already been computed before.}
